## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of per-tenant data isolation, retention controls, and safe data purging in a live streaming system. It requires concrete API shape, data model decisions, and a practical testing strategy suitable for beginners while exposing real trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped retention policy and TTL
- Message metadata and tombstone semantics for replay
- Purge safety relative to consumer offsets
- Canary-based testing and end-to-end validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: set TTL for a tenant-topic
async function setTTL(tenantId, topic, ttlMs) {
  // persist TTL in config store per tenant-topic
  await configStore.set(`/tenants/${tenantId}/topics/${topic}/ttl`, ttlMs);
}

// Pseudo: purge loop (simplified)
function purgeOldMessages(tenantId, topic) {
  const ttlMs = configStore.get(`/tenants/${tenantId}/topics/${topic}/ttl`);
  const cutoff = Date.now() - ttlMs;
  for (const msg of storage.scan(tenantId, topic)) {
    if (msg.timestamp < cutoff) {
      storage.delete(msg.id);
      // emit tombstone to preserve replay semantics
      storage.appendTombstone({ tenantId, topic, id: msg.id });
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle TTL changes mid-flight without losing data consistency?
- How do you verify no live consumers are affected during purge windows?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a scalable, privacy-preserving per-tenant DP aggregator in a streaming KCNA pipeline, including budget accounting, isolation, and test strategy for real-world fleet telemetry.

## Key Concepts
- Differential privacy in streaming; per-tenant budgeting; windowing; sensitivity; noise distribution; composition.
- Data model: tenant-id, vehicle readings, hourly windows, sums/counted values.
- System design: sandboxed operators, per-tenant isolation, watermarking, backpressure, fault tolerance.
- Testing: DP correctness, privacy accounting, performance under burst, end-to-end latency.

## Code Example
```javascript
function laplace(scale) {
  const u = Math.random() - 0.5;
  return -scale * Math.sign(u) * Math.log(1 - 2 * Math.abs(u));
}
function addDpNoise(sum, count, epsilon) {
  const noisySum = sum + laplace(1 / epsilon);
  const noisyCount = count + Math.max(1, Math.round(laplace(1 / epsilon)));
  return noisySum / Math.max(1, noisyCount);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt epsilon per-tenant based on usage or SLAs? 
- How would you verify privacy loss in production with audit trails and budget exhaustion alerts?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to apply backpressure and lag-based throttling in a practical Kafka scenario, evaluating skills in observability, dynamic resource tuning, and safe processing under bursty workloads.

## Key Concepts
- Per-partition lag measurement with rolling window statistics
- Dynamic throttling and in-flight request control
- Canary-style test design with simulated traffic bursts
- Observability: lag metrics, throughput monitoring, and alert thresholds

## Code Example
```javascript
class LagController {
  constructor(targetLag = 1000, windowSize = 10) {
    this.targetLag = targetLag;
    this.windowSize = windowSize;
    this.lagWindow = new Map(); // partition -> lag samples[]
    this.inFlight = new Map();  // partition -> current in-flight permits
  }

  recordLag(partition, lag) {
    if (!this.lagWindow.has(partition)) {
      this.lagWindow.set(partition, []);
    }
    const samples = this.lagWindow.get(partition);
    samples.push(lag);
    if (samples.length > this.windowSize) samples.shift();
  }

  getAverageLag(partition) {
    const samples = this.lagWindow.get(partition) || [];
    return samples.length ? samples.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / samples.length : 0;
  }

  shouldThrottle(partition) {
    return this.getAverageLag(partition) > this.targetLag;
  }

  adjustThrottle(partition) {
    const avgLag = this.getAverageLag(partition);
    const currentInFlight = this.inFlight.get(partition) || 10;

    if (avgLag > this.targetLag) {
      // Reduce fetch rate: decrease in-flight permits
      const reduced = Math.max(1, Math.floor(currentInFlight * 0.8));
      this.inFlight.set(partition, reduced);
    } else {
      // Ramp up slowly when lag is healthy
      const increased = Math.min(100, Math.floor(currentInFlight * 1.2));
      this.inFlight.set(partition, increased);
    }
    return this.inFlight.get(partition);
  }
}

// Minimal canary test
async function canaryTest() {
  const controller = new LagController(1000);
  const partitions = [0, 1, 2, 3];

  // Simulate burst: spike lag across all partitions
  for (const p of partitions) {
    controller.recordLag(p, 5000); // burst above target
  }

  // Verify throttling triggers
  const allThrottled = partitions.every(p => controller.shouldThrottle(p));
  console.log(`Throttle triggered: ${allThrottled}`);

  // Adjust and verify permits decrease
  partitions.forEach(p => {
    const permits = controller.adjustThrottle(p);
    console.log(`Partition ${p}: permits = ${permits}`);
  });
}
```

## Expected Answer Elements
- **Data structures**: Lag sample window per partition, in-flight permit tracker, target threshold
- **Triggers**: Timer-based lag sampling, configurable poll interval, lag spike detection
- **Canary test**: Burst generator simulating traffic spike, assertion that lag stabilizes within target
